# Copy to .env and fill in. .env is ignored by git and is never shown to the model.

# From @BotFather in Telegram: /newbot
TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN=
# Your own numeric Telegram user ID. Only this account gets replies.
OWNER_TELEGRAM_ID=
# From the Claude Console
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=
# From exa.ai
EXA_API_KEY=

# Optional
# ODYSSEUS_MODEL=claude-sonnet-5-5
# ODYSSEUS_MAX_TURNS=25
# ODYSSEUS_TASK_USD=0.50
# ODYSSEUS_DAILY_USD=3.00

# Optional: from the Composio dashboard. Without it, mail and calendar are simply not there.
# COMPOSIO_API_KEY=
# Optional: pin the clock to a zone, e.g. Asia/Kolkata, when running on a server elsewhere.
# ODYSSEUS_TZ=
# Optional: from cloud.browser-use.com. Without it, there is no browser.
# BROWSER_USE_API_KEY=
# Optional: from console.mistral.ai. Without it, files you send cannot be read.
# MISTRAL_API_KEY=
# Optional: an email address of the assistant's own, to copy on threads and for founders to send decks to. Create an
# account at agentmail.to and put its API key here, then send /address in the chat. One inbox per person.
# AGENTMAIL_API_KEY=
# Optional: lets the assistant run code (spreadsheets, arithmetic, charts) on a throwaway machine with no internet.
# Create an account at e2b.dev and put its API key here. The code and any file it is given pass through E2B.
# E2B_API_KEY=
# Your other addresses, comma separated, besides the mailbox you connect. Mail from these to the assistant is dealt
# with at once; mail from anyone else is put to you first.
# ODYSSEUS_OWNER_EMAILS=
# A browser run that is signed in as you asks you first. Set to 0 to let such runs start without asking.
# ODYSSEUS_BROWSER_ASK_SIGNED_IN=
# Small changes that can be put back run without asking and are listed in the reply: additions to Attio, additions and
# edits in your own Google files, a routine set up from your own words. Set to 1 to be asked before every change.
# ODYSSEUS_ASK_EVERY_CHANGE=
# A deck you send by itself, a document from a link, and a filing you approve from a question are dealt with in the
# background while you go on talking. The number is how many such jobs may run at once; each is one more agent kept
# ready, which takes memory. 0 goes back to one thing at a time.
# ODYSSEUS_BACKGROUND_JOBS=2
# Each morning, at this time on your clock, you are asked about people you met or heard from who are not in your
# database yet; one tap files them. Costs nothing unless you tap. "off" switches it off.
# ODYSSEUS_CAPTURE_AT=08:30
# Before a document is sent, its pages are read against your request by a model that did not write it. Empty: the
# background model. ODYSSEUS_REVIEW_MODEL=off switches this off together with the second reading of outgoing mail.
# ODYSSEUS_PAGES_CHECK_MODEL=
# The pipeline review: the weekday and time it is sent by itself, once a week. No model is used. "off": only when asked for.
# ODYSSEUS_PIPELINE_REVIEW_AT=mon 08:00
# Each morning at this time your inbox is searched, by code, for updates from companies on a list named portfolio in
# your database. Each one found is read and its figures kept, which uses the model. "off" switches it off.
# ODYSSEUS_UPDATES_AT=08:45
# People you keep in touch with: each working day at this time you are asked about the ones that are due, and a
# colleague whose job one is gets a reminder by email until they reply. No model is used. "off" switches it off.
# ODYSSEUS_CHECKINS_AT=09:30
# With a check-in of yours that is due, and when you ask, it names up to three people worth asking that person about,
# from one web search run on the main model (under ten cents, plus the searches). 0 switches it off.
# ODYSSEUS_SUGGEST_PEOPLE=1
# An email, reply or invitation is read once more by a small model before you are asked to approve it, and a line is
# added under the question if something looks wrong. It cannot approve anything. Empty: the background model.
# "off" switches the second reading off.
# ODYSSEUS_REVIEW_MODEL=
# The model that looks at the pages of a PDF you send (charts, logos). Empty: the main model.
# ODYSSEUS_PAGES_MODEL=
# After this many minutes without a message, the next one starts a new conversation that carries the last few
# exchanges and your memory. Keeps each message cheap. 0 keeps one conversation for ever.
# ODYSSEUS_FRESH_AFTER_MIN=120
# The address given to DocSend when it asks who is viewing. Empty: the address of the mailbox you connected.
# A verification email can only be answered if this is that mailbox.
# ODYSSEUS_VIEWER_EMAIL=
# A short brief is sent half an hour before each meeting with someone from outside your firm. Set to 0 to turn it off.
# ODYSSEUS_MEETING_BRIEFS=
# Optional: record every task as a trace in Langfuse, to read real use and find failures. Both keys are needed.
# Note what this sends: your messages, the replies, and what tools returned, including the text of mail that was read.
# LANGFUSE_PUBLIC_KEY=
# LANGFUSE_SECRET_KEY=
# LANGFUSE_BASE_URL=
# Optional: set to 1 to also record the text that mail, calendar and app tools returned. Off, only its length is recorded.
# LANGFUSE_FULL_CONTENT=

# Optional: WhatsApp as a second channel, through Meta's WhatsApp Cloud API. See the deploy guide.
# It needs the program to be reachable from the internet over HTTPS at /whatsapp, which a laptop is not.
# WHATSAPP_TOKEN=
# WHATSAPP_PHONE_ID=
# WHATSAPP_APP_SECRET=
# WHATSAPP_VERIFY_TOKEN=
# Your own WhatsApp number with country code, digits only. Only this number is listened to.
# OWNER_WHATSAPP=
# WHATSAPP_PORT=8080
# Where reminders and routines are sent: telegram (default) or whatsapp.
# ODYSSEUS_NOTIFY_ON=

# Optional: iMessage as a third channel, bought from Linq (linqapp.com). It needs the same public HTTPS address as
# WhatsApp, at /linq. Put the account's token, your Linq number and your own number here, then run
# python scripts/connect_linq.py once: it registers the address with Linq and writes the signing secret below.
# The channel only answers; reminders stay on Telegram. A free Linq line can write only to someone who wrote first.
# LINQ_API_TOKEN=
# LINQ_NUMBER=
# OWNER_IMESSAGE=
# LINQ_WEBHOOK_SECRET=
# Set to off to switch iMessage off without removing the four lines above.
# ODYSSEUS_IMESSAGE=

# Where the journal, memory, conversation and schedule are kept. They hold mail text in the clear, so keep this
# out of any folder that syncs to a cloud drive (OneDrive, Dropbox, iCloud). The program warns at start if it is in one.
# ODYSSEUS_DATA_DIR=var
# More than one person on this copy. You are the host: send /invite in the chat for a link that lets one person in.
# Each person gets a folder, memory, sign-ins and daily limit of their own. You hold the keys and pay for everyone.
# ODYSSEUS_PEOPLE_MAX=10
# ODYSSEUS_MEMBER_DAILY_USD=3.00
# An invited person who has sent nothing for this many minutes keeps everything and loses only their agents' processes,
# which are started again by their next message. 0: never.
# ODYSSEUS_PARK_AFTER_MIN=15
# On a server: the https address at which the program's port (ODYSSEUS_PORT, default 8080) is reachable. With it, a
# sign-in to Attio or another service ends on a page that says Connected, and nobody has to copy an address into the chat.
# ODYSSEUS_PUBLIC_URL=https://your-app.fly.dev
# Optional: to let the assistant read X. Create an app at developer.x.com (OAuth 2.0, native app, read permission, return
# address http://127.0.0.1:8765/callback), put its client id here, then run: python scripts/connect_mcp.py x
# X_CLIENT_ID=
